<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Van Lang</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 258 BCE (approx.) · capital Phong Chau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The semi-legendary Bronze Age realm of the Hung kings in the Red River delta, honoured in Vietnam as the first Vietnamese state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Vietnamese tradition, first written down in medieval works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linh-nam-chich-quai">Linh Nam chich quai</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-viet-su-ky-toan-thu">Dai Viet su ky toan thu</a>, traces Van Lang to the Hong Bang dynasty founded in 2879 BCE. Historians link it instead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Dong Son culture</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-asia">Red River</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-river">Ma River</a> valleys, which flourished from about the 7th century BCE, with its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phong-chau">Phong Chau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Hung kings are said to have ruled through lords called Lac hau and Lac tuong over fifteen territories, a picture that suggests a chiefdom or loose confederation rather than a bureaucratic state. Its people grew wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and cast the famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Dong Son</a> bronze drums, decorated with boats, birds and warriors, which spread across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to tradition, the last Hung king was overthrown in 258 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-duong-vuong">Thuc Phan</a>, who united Van Lang with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/au-viet">Au Viet</a> to form <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/au-lac">Au Lac</a>. The Hung kings are still venerated at the temple on Nghia Linh mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phu-tho-province">Phu Tho</a>, and their death anniversary is a national holiday in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/van-lang.jpg" alt="Van Lang" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: DannamEmpire · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%E1%BB%93ng_B%C3%A0ng_Dynasty_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 258 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phong Chau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor and spirit worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Proto-Vietic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vietnam</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Văn Lang, Hong Bang dynasty, Hồng Bàng</dd></dl></aside></div>
